  "enhanced_information": "The 67-year-old man presents with symptoms suggestive of multiple myeloma, indicated by low hemoglobin, elevated creatinine, and a peripheral blood smear showing stacks of red blood cells (rouleaux formation). Serum electrophoresis revealing an abnormal protein spike supports this diagnosis. In multiple myeloma, Bence-Jones proteins (light chains of immunoglobulins) are typically found in the urine due to the overproduction of monoclonal proteins by malignant plasma cells. Therefore, the most expected additional finding in this patient would be the presence of Bence-Jones proteins in the urine, aligning with the consensus on multiple myeloma diagnostics."
